---
components: ["PageHeader", "PageHeaderSkeleton"]
description: Page headers identify the current page with a title and subtitle, with optional breadcrumb, tabs, and page actions.
---

<script>
  import {
    PageHeader,
    PageHeaderSkeleton,
    Breadcrumb,
    BreadcrumbItem,
    Tabs,
    Tab,
    TabContent,
    ButtonSet,
    Button,
    Box,
    Text,
    Link,
    Tag,
    IconIndicator,
    UserAvatar,
    OverflowMenu,
    OverflowMenuItem,
    ComboButton,
    MenuButton,
    MenuItem,
    MenuDivider,
    Search,
    ContainedList,
    ContainedListItem,
    Row,
    Column,
    Tile,
    BigNumber,
    DescriptionList,
    DescriptionListItem,
    MenuItemRadioGroup,
  } from "carbon-components-svelte";
  import Download from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Download.svelte";
  import Renew from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Renew.svelte";
  import Add from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Add.svelte";
  import DataBase from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/DataBase.svelte";
  import Copy from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Copy.svelte";
  import Edit from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Edit.svelte";
  import Folder from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Folder.svelte";
  import Share from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Share.svelte";
  import Star from "carbon-icons-svelte/lib/Star.svelte";

  const databases = [
    { name: "db-prod-01", engine: "PostgreSQL 16", region: "us-south" },
    { name: "db-prod-02", engine: "PostgreSQL 16", region: "eu-de" },
    { name: "analytics", engine: "MySQL 8", region: "us-east" },
    { name: "cache-sessions", engine: "Redis 7", region: "us-south" },
  ];

  const timeRanges = [
    { id: "1h", text: "Last hour" },
    { id: "24h", text: "Last 24 hours" },
    { id: "7d", text: "Last 7 days" },
  ];

  let timeRange = "24h";

  $: timeRangeLabel = timeRanges.find((range) => range.id === timeRange)?.text;

  let databaseFilter = "";

  $: filteredDatabases = databases.filter((db) =>
    [db.name, db.engine, db.region].some((field) =>
      field.toLowerCase().includes(databaseFilter.trim().toLowerCase()),
    ),
  );
</script>

## Basic

Set `titleText` and `subtitle`. The title renders as a `Heading`, so its level follows the nearest `Section` (an `h1` by default).

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances" />

## Breadcrumb

Place a `Breadcrumb` in the `breadcrumb` snippet to render it above the title.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet breadcrumb()}
    <Breadcrumb noTrailingSlash>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/">Home</BreadcrumbItem>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/">Resources</BreadcrumbItem>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/" isCurrentPage>Databases</BreadcrumbItem>
    </Breadcrumb>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Breadcrumb actions

Use the `breadcrumbEnd` snippet for small, page-wide actions such as share or favorite. They sit at the end of the breadcrumb row, away from the main page actions.

<PageHeader titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet breadcrumb()}
    <Breadcrumb noTrailingSlash>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/">Home</BreadcrumbItem>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/">Databases</BreadcrumbItem>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/" isCurrentPage>db-prod-01</BreadcrumbItem>
    </Breadcrumb>
  {/snippet}
  {#snippet breadcrumbEnd()}
    <Button kind="ghost" size="small" icon={Share} iconDescription="Share" />
    <Button kind="ghost" size="small" icon={Star} iconDescription="Add to favorites" />
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

## Tabs and panels

### Tabs

Place a `Tabs` in the `tabs` snippet to render page-level navigation along the header's bottom edge.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet tabs()}
    <Tabs>
      <Tab label="Overview" />
      <Tab label="Backups" />
      <Tab label="Settings" />
    </Tabs>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Tab panels

Content in `children` renders after the header, outside the `<header>` element. Place one `TabContent` per tab there to pair each panel with its tab in the `tabs` snippet, as you would in the `Tabs` `content` snippet. Panels stay out of the header, so a `sticky` header does not pin them.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet tabs()}
    <Tabs>
      <Tab label="Overview" />
      <Tab label="Backups" />
      <Tab label="Settings" />
    </Tabs>
  {/snippet}
  <TabContent>Overview: 4 instances, 2 regions.</TabContent>
  <TabContent>Backups: nightly, kept for 30 days.</TabContent>
  <TabContent>Settings: maintenance window Sunday 02:00 UTC.</TabContent>
</PageHeader>

### Tabs end

Use the `tabsEnd` snippet for controls that apply to every tab, such as a time range. They sit at the end of the tabs row, lined up with the actions. A ghost `MenuButton` matches the tab height, and `intrinsicAlign="end"` opens its menu toward the page.

<PageHeader titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet tabs()}
    <Tabs>
      <Tab label="Overview" />
      <Tab label="Metrics" />
      <Tab label="Logs" />
    </Tabs>
  {/snippet}
  {#snippet tabsEnd()}
    <MenuButton kind="ghost" intrinsicAlign="end" labelText={timeRangeLabel}>
      <MenuItemRadioGroup labelText="Time range" bind:selectedId={timeRange}>
        {#each timeRanges as range (range.id)}
          <MenuItem id={range.id} labelText={range.text} />
        {/each}
      </MenuItemRadioGroup>
    </MenuButton>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Container tabs

Set `type="container"` on the `Tabs` in `tabs` to use container tabs. The header drops its divider, and panels in `children` take the `"layer-01"` surface of the selected tab.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet tabs()}
    <Tabs type="container">
      <Tab label="Overview" />
      <Tab label="Backups" />
      <Tab label="Settings" />
    </Tabs>
  {/snippet}
  <TabContent>Overview: 4 instances, 2 regions.</TabContent>
  <TabContent>Backups: nightly, kept for 30 days.</TabContent>
  <TabContent>Settings: maintenance window Sunday 02:00 UTC.</TabContent>
</PageHeader>

On a layered header (`fill="layer-01"`), the selected tab and its panel switch to the page `"background"` instead, so they still stand apart from the header.

<PageHeader fill="layer-01" titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet tabs()}
    <Tabs type="container">
      <Tab label="Overview" />
      <Tab label="Backups" />
      <Tab label="Settings" />
    </Tabs>
  {/snippet}
  <TabContent>Overview: 4 instances, 2 regions.</TabContent>
  <TabContent>Backups: nightly, kept for 30 days.</TabContent>
  <TabContent>Settings: maintenance window Sunday 02:00 UTC.</TabContent>
</PageHeader>

## Actions

The `actions` snippet aligns page actions with the title and wraps them below it on narrow screens. Children sit side by side with no gap, like a `ButtonSet`, so wrap them in a `Stack` or `Box` when they need space between them. Match control heights, for example `size="lg"` on `ComboButton` and `MenuButton` and `size="xl"` on `OverflowMenu`, to line up with default-size buttons.

### Single action

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet actions()}
    <Button icon={Add}>Create database</Button>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Button set

Place a `ButtonSet` or `ActionSet` in the snippet for a secondary and a primary action. Each button sizes to its label.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet actions()}
    <ButtonSet>
      <Button kind="secondary">Import</Button>
      <Button icon={Add}>Create database</Button>
    </ButtonSet>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Icon buttons

Pair ghost icon-only buttons with the primary action for frequent secondary tasks.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet actions()}
    <Button kind="ghost" icon={Renew} iconDescription="Refresh" tooltipPosition="bottom" />
    <Button kind="ghost" icon={Download} iconDescription="Export" tooltipPosition="bottom" />
    <Button icon={Add}>Create database</Button>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Overflow menu

Move less frequent actions into an `OverflowMenu` beside the primary action. Set `flipped` so the menu opens toward the page, not off its edge.

<PageHeader titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet actions()}
    <ButtonSet>
      <Button kind="secondary">Restart</Button>
      <Button>Connect</Button>
    </ButtonSet>
    <OverflowMenu size="xl" flipped iconDescription="More actions">
      <OverflowMenuItem text="Rename" />
      <OverflowMenuItem text="Clone" />
      <OverflowMenuItem danger text="Delete" />
    </OverflowMenu>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Combo button

Use a `ComboButton` when one action is most common and related variants belong in its menu. Set `tooltipAlignment="end"` so the trigger's tooltip stays on screen at the page's right edge.

<PageHeader titleText="Deployments" subtitle="Release builds to your environments">
  {#snippet actions()}
    <ComboButton
      size="lg"
      labelText="Deploy"
      iconDescription="More deploy options"
      tooltipAlignment="end"
    >
      <MenuItem>Deploy to staging</MenuItem>
      <MenuItem>Deploy to production</MenuItem>
      <MenuDivider />
      <MenuItem>Schedule deploy</MenuItem>
    </ComboButton>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Menu button

Use a `MenuButton` to group several actions of equal weight behind one trigger. Set `intrinsicAlign="end"` so the menu opens aligned to the trigger's right edge, toward the page.

<PageHeader titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet actions()}
    <MenuButton size="lg" kind="tertiary" labelText="Actions" intrinsicAlign="end">
      <MenuItem>Restart</MenuItem>
      <MenuItem>Create backup</MenuItem>
      <MenuItem>Scale</MenuItem>
      <MenuDivider />
      <MenuItem kind="danger">Delete</MenuItem>
    </MenuButton>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Filter

Place a `Search` in the snippet to filter the content below the header in place. Wrap it in a `Box` with a fixed `width`, since a `Search` otherwise shrinks to its placeholder in the actions row.

<PageHeader
  titleText="Databases"
  subtitle="{filteredDatabases.length} of {databases.length} instances"
>
  {#snippet actions()}
    <Box width="20rem">
      <Search
        labelText="Filter databases"
        placeholder="Filter by name, engine, or region"
        bind:value={databaseFilter}
      />
    </Box>
  {/snippet}
  <ContainedList labelText="Instances" hideLabel>
    {#each filteredDatabases as db (db.name)}
      <ContainedListItem>{db.name} · {db.engine} · {db.region}</ContainedListItem>
    {:else}
      <ContainedListItem>No databases match "{databaseFilter}".</ContainedListItem>
    {/each}
  </ContainedList>
</PageHeader>

## Title content

### Title only

Omit `subtitle` for a title-only header.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" />

### Custom title

Pass a snippet to `titleText` for rich title content. It renders inside the heading. Place a `Text` with `tag="span"` in the snippet to give the title a different type style.

<PageHeader subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet titleText()}
    <Text tag="span" type="heading-03">Databases</Text>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Title icon

Use the `titleStart` snippet to place an icon before the title.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet titleStart()}
    <DataBase size={24} aria-hidden="true" />
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Eyebrow

Set `eyebrow` for short text above the title, such as the parent section or resource type.

<PageHeader eyebrow="Resources" titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances" />

### Eyebrow with icon

Set `eyebrowIcon` to place an icon before the eyebrow text. The icon always renders at 16px, whatever size the icon component is given.

<PageHeader
  eyebrow="Resources"
  eyebrowIcon={Folder}
  titleText="Databases"
  subtitle="Manage your database instances"
/>

### Avatar

Place a `UserAvatar` in the `titleStart` snippet for a person or account page.

<PageHeader titleText="Richard Hendricks" subtitle="Administrator · richard.hendricks@example.com">
  {#snippet titleStart()}
    <UserAvatar
      name="Richard Hendricks"
      image="https://i.pravatar.cc/300?img=12"
      imageDescription="Richard Hendricks"
    />
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Status

Use the `titleEnd` snippet for a status `Tag` or `IconIndicator` beside the title.

<PageHeader titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet titleEnd()}
    <Tag type="green" size="sm">Running</Tag>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

<PageHeader titleText="Nightly backup" subtitle="Last run 2 hours ago">
  {#snippet titleEnd()}
    <IconIndicator kind="in-progress" label="Running" />
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Contextual actions

Use the `titleEnd` snippet for small ghost icon buttons that act on the title itself, such as rename or copy. Keep page-wide actions in the `actions` snippet.

<PageHeader titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet titleEnd()}
    <Button kind="ghost" size="small" icon={Edit} iconDescription="Rename" />
    <Button kind="ghost" size="small" icon={Copy} iconDescription="Copy name" />
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Description

Use the `description` snippet for longer rich text, such as a link to more help, in place of a one-line `subtitle`.

<PageHeader titleText="Databases">
  {#snippet description()}
    Provision, scale, and back up managed PostgreSQL and MySQL instances.
    Backups run nightly and are kept for 30 days. <Link href="/">Learn about backups</Link>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Metadata

Use the `meta` snippet for key facts between the title and the tabs, such as a `DescriptionList` with `columns`.

<PageHeader titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet titleEnd()}
    <Tag type="green" size="sm">Running</Tag>
  {/snippet}
  {#snippet meta()}
    <DescriptionList columns={4}>
      <DescriptionListItem term="Region">us-south</DescriptionListItem>
      <DescriptionListItem term="Plan">Standard</DescriptionListItem>
      <DescriptionListItem term="Storage">42 GB</DescriptionListItem>
      <DescriptionListItem term="Created">March 14, 2026</DescriptionListItem>
    </DescriptionList>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

## Appearance

### Fill

Set `fill` to `"layer-01"` or `"layer-02"` to set the header apart from the page body. The default is `"background"`.

<PageHeader fill="layer-01" titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
  {#snippet breadcrumb()}
    <Breadcrumb noTrailingSlash>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/">Home</BreadcrumbItem>
      <BreadcrumbItem href="/" isCurrentPage>Databases</BreadcrumbItem>
    </Breadcrumb>
  {/snippet}
  {#snippet actions()}
    <Button icon={Add}>Create database</Button>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Small

Set `size` to `"sm"` for a compact header on nested or detail pages. It uses a smaller title and tighter vertical spacing, and keeps the same inline padding so the title stays aligned.

<PageHeader size="sm" titleText="Edit rule" subtitle="Alert rules / CPU above 90%">
  {#snippet actions()}
    <Button kind="secondary" size="field">Cancel</Button>
    <Button size="field">Save</Button>
  {/snippet}
</PageHeader>

### Hidden divider

Set `hideDivider` to `true` to hide the border below the header, such as when the content below already starts with its own edge.

<PageHeader hideDivider titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances" />

## Skeleton

Use `PageHeaderSkeleton` while the page's data loads. It renders a title and subtitle placeholder by default; set `breadcrumb`, `actions`, and `tabs` (a count) to match the header it stands in for, and set `grid`, `fill`, and `hideDivider` the same way so it swaps in without shifting the layout.

<PageHeaderSkeleton />

<PageHeaderSkeleton breadcrumb actions tabs={3} />

## Grid alignment

Set `grid` to line the header up with a `Grid`. The header content and the page body in `children` both become grid containers, with a `Grid`'s max width, centering, and breakpoint padding, so the title lines up with each `Column`'s content. Place `Row`s in the body directly, without another `Grid`. Set `grid` to `"full-width"` to match `Grid fullWidth`.

<PageHeader grid titleText="db-prod-01" subtitle="PostgreSQL 16 · us-south">
  {#snippet tabs()}
    <Tabs>
      <Tab label="Overview" />
      <Tab label="Metrics" />
    </Tabs>
  {/snippet}
  <TabContent>
    <Row>
      <Column><Tile><BigNumber labelText="Connections" value={128} /></Tile></Column>
      <Column><Tile><BigNumber labelText="Queries per second" value={2400} /></Tile></Column>
      <Column><Tile><BigNumber labelText="Replicas" value={3} /></Tile></Column>
    </Row>
  </TabContent>
  <TabContent>
    <Row>
      <Column><Text>Metrics charts go here.</Text></Column>
    </Row>
  </TabContent>
</PageHeader>

In grid mode, default-tab panels drop their inline padding so a `Row` in them lines up with the title. Container-tab panels keep it and line up with the container tab labels instead.

## Sticky

Set `sticky` to `true` to pin the header to the top of its nearest scroll container while the content below it scrolls.

<Box height="20rem" border="subtle" overflow="auto">
  <PageHeader sticky titleText="Databases" subtitle="Manage your database instances">
    {#snippet breadcrumb()}
      <Breadcrumb noTrailingSlash>
        <BreadcrumbItem href="/">Home</BreadcrumbItem>
        <BreadcrumbItem href="/" isCurrentPage>Databases</BreadcrumbItem>
      </Breadcrumb>
    {/snippet}
    {#snippet tabs()}
      <Tabs>
        <Tab label="Overview" />
        <Tab label="Backups" />
      </Tabs>
    {/snippet}
    <TabContent>
      <Box height="40rem">
        <Text>Scroll this area; the header and its tabs stay pinned to the top.</Text>
      </Box>
    </TabContent>
    <TabContent>
      <Box height="40rem">
        <Text>Backups panel. The header stays pinned here too.</Text>
      </Box>
    </TabContent>
  </PageHeader>
</Box>

### Sticky offset

Set `stickyOffset` to stick the header below other fixed chrome, such as `"3rem"` to clear a UI Shell `Header`. Numbers `0`–`13` use the shared layout scale; strings accept any CSS length. Scroll the frame below: the page header stays under the app header. The example removes `Content`'s padding so the header spans the page.

<FileSource src="/framed/PageHeader/StickyUIShell" />

## Page layouts

Full pages built from `PageHeader` with `grid`, so the header lines up with the `Row`s in its body.

### Resource detail

A detail page with a breadcrumb, an eyebrow, a status tag, key facts in `meta`, a `ComboButton`, and tabs whose panels hold `Tile`s and a `DataTable`.

<FileSource src="/framed/PageHeader/ResourceDetail" />

### List page

A list page with the instance count in the subtitle and a create action. Filtering lives in the `DataTable` toolbar, next to the rows it filters.

<FileSource src="/framed/PageHeader/ListPage" />

### Settings page

A compact (`size="sm"`) header over a two-column body: section links on the left and the form on the right.

<FileSource src="/framed/PageHeader/SettingsPage" />

### Dashboard

A layered header (`fill="layer-01"`) with a time range in `tabsEnd` that applies to every tab.

<FileSource src="/framed/PageHeader/Dashboard" />

## Patterns

### Responsive actions

Use `Breakpoint` to swap a `ButtonSet` for an `OverflowMenu` on small screens. Narrow the window to see the actions collapse below the `lg` breakpoint.

<FileSource src="/framed/PageHeader/ResponsiveActions" />

### Tabs in the URL

Bind the header's `Tabs` to `queryParam` so the selected tab survives a reload and can be linked to. The body panels follow the selection as usual.

<FileSource src="/framed/PageHeader/QueryStringTabs" />

